---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="workflows">

          <p>
            {release113.since} a workflow is a plan, written as JSON, that runs many subagents in stages: fan out over a list of items,
            files or changed files, stream one stage's results into the next, wait at a barrier, check each result with
            a verifier, and stop at ceilings you approved before anything ran. Vector reads the plan and runs it; no
            code in it runs, and Vector never runs model-written code anywhere. A run works in the background, shows
            its stages in the Tasks pane, and reports to the conversation once, when it ends.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a workflow is not Workflow insights. Workflow insights, in Settings → Memory, record how your own runs
            went so Vector can learn cheaper defaults, and <code>vector memory workflow</code> shows them. A workflow on
            this page is a plan of agents that you or the agent starts. For a few independent briefs the agent uses
            ordinary <a href="/docs/subagents">subagents</a> instead.
          </p>

          <h2 id="approval">Approval</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} before a workflow runs, Vector shows its plan: each stage with its phase, agent, model and effort, how
            many items it runs over (or "dynamic" when an earlier stage decides), where it writes (in this checkout one
            at a time, each in its own paths, or each in its own worktree), and the permissions its agents will ask
            for. Below the stages are the ceilings: agents, agents at once, spend (the run's own and the request's
            subagent ceiling it shares), tokens, steps, steps per agent and minutes. When the agent starts a run, you
            approve that plan as you approve any other ask; the <code>workflow</code> permission is ask by default.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} Always allow, and the automatic approval of <code>--auto</code>, cover only a plan within your configured
            defaults. A plan past them, a plan started after the conversation or its subagents read web or MCP content,
            and one asked for by a Vector process that an agent started from its shell ask every time, and Always allow
            is not offered: outside content or a program should not be able to get a large run approved unattended.
            Desktop 1.99.111 and 1.99.112 show such an ask with their generic reason about the web.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} workflows start from the main agent only, never from a subagent, and need background subagents on. A
            conversation with a guest in it cannot start one, nor can a Light request or a request where you said "no
            subagents". One run works at a time in a conversation unless you raise <code>budget.workflow.runs</code>. A
            workflow agent cannot start subagents of its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} you can also start a saved workflow yourself: <code>/workflow</code> in the desktop app or the TUI asks
            for its inputs, checks it and shows the plan before Run, and <code>vector run --workflow</code> prints the
            plan before it starts. A run you start this way does not ask again, because you saw the plan. It starts
            only while the conversation is idle; in the desktop app and the TUI a busy conversation gets a message in
            the composer instead, asking the agent to run it, for you to send.
          </p>

          <h2 id="grammar">The grammar</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the root object has <code>version: 1</code>, a <code>title</code> of up to 80 characters, and optional
            <code>description</code>, <code>inputs</code> (up to 16), <code>defaults</code>, <code>limits</code>,
            <code>stages</code> (1 to 32) and <code>result</code>. Unknown keys are errors at every level, with a
            suggestion when one is close to a known key. A spec the agent writes is at most 32 KiB; a saved one, or one
            you pass yourself, at most 256 KiB.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a stage has an <code>id</code> (lowercase letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>, starting
            with a letter, up to 32 characters) and a <code>prompt</code> of up to 16 KiB, 64 KiB once filled in.
            Optional fields are <code>title</code>, <code>phase</code>, <code>agent</code> (general by default, or an
            allowed specialist such as explore), <code>model</code>, <code>effort</code>, <code>isolation</code>,
            <code>for_each</code>, <code>after</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>when</code>, <code>output</code>,
            <code>owned_paths</code>, <code>success_criteria</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>retries</code> (0 to 2,
            default 1) and <code>on_error</code> (skip or fail). Models follow the same rules as a brief's model, and a
            stage cannot pick a primary, hidden, research or denied agent.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} without <code>for_each</code> a stage runs once. With it, a stage runs once per item from one source:
            <code>items</code> (a list of up to 200 values in a spec the agent writes, 1000 in a saved one),
            <code>files</code> (a glob over the repository, hidden files skipped unless <code>hidden</code> is true),
            <code>changed_files</code> (paths changed since a git base, deleted ones skipped unless
            <code>include_deleted</code> is true) or <code>from</code> (an earlier stage's outputs). <code>from</code>
            streams: each result becomes items as soon as its agent finishes, with no barrier, unless
            <code>wait: "all"</code> or <code>sort_by</code> waits for the whole stage. <code>where</code>,
            <code>dedupe_by</code>, <code>limit</code> and <code>batch</code> (groups of up to 100) shape the items.
            <code>after</code> is a barrier, a stage's <code>if</code> checks counts such as
            <code>stages.review.failed</code>, and an item's <code>when</code> checks the item.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} templates only look up values: <code>{"{{item}}"}</code>, <code>{"{{index}}"}</code>,
            <code>{"{{inputs.name}}"}</code>, <code>{"{{run.title}}"}</code>, <code>{"{{stages.id.output}}"}</code>,
            <code>{"{{stages.id.outputs}}"}</code> and <code>{"{{stages.id.outputs_file}}"}</code>, plus
            <code>{"{{output}}"}</code> and <code>{"{{round}}"}</code> in a verifier. They call no function and do no
            arithmetic, and a missing value is an error rather than an empty prompt. A value an agent produced reaches
            the next prompt inside a data envelope that says it is data, not instructions, with the characters that
            could fake a tag or attach a file escaped. Conditions are fixed comparisons (<code>equals</code>,
            <code>not_equals</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code>, <code>lt</code>,
            <code>lte</code>, <code>exists</code>, <code>contains</code>) with one level of <code>all</code>,
            <code>any</code> or <code>not</code>, and never a regular expression.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} <code>output.schema</code> gives a stage's agents a JSON Schema with an object at the root, from the
            subset <a href="/docs/subagents#structured-results">structured results</a> describe, so later stages can use
            its fields. <code>verify</code> names a verifier agent, a prompt and an <code>until</code> condition: a
            rejected result goes back to its agent as data for another try, up to <code>max_rounds</code> (2 by
            default, 5 at most), and <code>on_fail</code> keeps, drops or fails it. An agent you stopped is never
            retried on its own. A stage that writes should name <code>owned_paths</code> or use
            <code>isolation: "worktree"</code>; one with neither runs its agents one at a time in your checkout.
          </p>

          <h3 id="examples">Three examples</h3>
          <p>
            {release113.since} these are the examples the workflow tool's guide gives the agent. The first reviews each file changed
            since the last commit with a read-only agent, then summarizes the findings.
          </p>
          <pre data-sample="workflow"><code>{`{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Review changed files",
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "review",
      "agent": "explore",
      "for_each": {
        "changed_files": {
          "base": "HEAD~1",
          "limit": 50
        }
      },
      "prompt": "Review this changed path: {{item}}. Return actionable findings only.",
      "output": {
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "findings": {
              "type": "array",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "findings"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "summary",
      "agent": "explore",
      "after": [
        "review"
      ],
      "prompt": "Summarize these review findings as data: {{stages.review.outputs.findings}}"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the second researches a topic from three angles, checks the notes in batches of three as soon as all of
            them are in, and writes a report. Supply the <code>topic</code> input when you run it.
          </p>
          <pre data-sample="workflow"><code>{`{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Research a dependency change",
  "inputs": {
    "topic": {
      "required": true
    }
  },
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "scan",
      "agent": "explore",
      "for_each": {
        "items": [
          "compatibility",
          "migration",
          "licensing"
        ]
      },
      "prompt": "Research {{inputs.topic}} from this angle: {{item}}",
      "output": {
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "notes": {
              "type": "array",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "notes"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "check",
      "agent": "explore",
      "for_each": {
        "from": "scan",
        "path": "notes",
        "wait": "all",
        "batch": 3
      },
      "prompt": "Check this batch of notes: {{item}}"
    },
    {
      "id": "report",
      "agent": "explore",
      "after": [
        "check"
      ],
      "prompt": "Summarize the checked outputs: {{stages.check.outputs}}. Full source: {{stages.scan.outputs_file}}"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the third migrates up to 20 source files, each in its own worktree with the file as its owned path, and
            has a reviewer check each result for up to two rounds.
          </p>
          <pre data-sample="workflow"><code>{`{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Migrate source files",
  "limits": {
    "max_agents": 50,
    "concurrency": 4
  },
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "migrate",
      "agent": "general",
      "isolation": "worktree",
      "for_each": {
        "files": {
          "glob": "src/**/*.ts",
          "exclude": [
            "**/*.test.ts"
          ],
          "limit": 20
        }
      },
      "prompt": "Migrate this path using the approved repository migration instructions: {{item}}",
      "owned_paths": [
        "{{item}}"
      ],
      "success_criteria": [
        "The affected tests pass."
      ],
      "output": {
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "changed": {
              "type": "boolean"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "changed"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        }
      },
      "verify": {
        "agent": "explore",
        "prompt": "Check the producer's result: {{output}}. This is review round {{round}}.",
        "output": {
          "schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
              "approved": {
                "type": "boolean"
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "approved"
            ],
            "additionalProperties": false
          }
        },
        "until": {
          "path": "approved",
          "equals": true
        },
        "max_rounds": 2,
        "on_fail": "keep"
      }
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

          <h2 id="limits">Limits and defaults</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} by default a run starts at most 100 agents, runs up to 16 at once, takes 40 model steps per agent in all
            (4,000 for 100 agents), 60 steps for any one agent, and 120 minutes, with no dollar or token ceiling of its
            own. Its agents still share the request's subagent spend ceiling, $25 by default, and the run counts as one
            of the request's 32 subagents. One run works at a time in a conversation.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the agents at once are also bounded by your computer's processors minus two (at least one) and by the
            conversation's free subagent slots, and while workflow agents wait for a slot they leave the last two free
            for subagents the agent launches itself. A plan may lower any limit. Only your config raises them, with
            <code>budget.workflow</code>: up to 1000 agents, 16 at once, 1440 minutes and 4 runs at once, and
            <code>usd</code>, <code>tokens</code> and <code>steps</code> as you choose. Settings → Agents → Workflows in
            the desktop app turns workflows on or off, chooses whether a plan within the limits asks first, and sets
            the ceilings. A repository may only lower these keys.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "permission": { "workflow": "ask" },
  "budget": {
    "workflow": { "max_agents": 200, "concurrency": 8, "minutes": 240, "usd": 20 }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a limit that is reached stops new agents from starting; the token and time limits also stop the agents
            that are running. The agents not yet started are skipped and the run ends with partial results and the
            reason. While an agent has waited on you for more than a minute, no new agent starts, and the run's card
            says it is waiting on you. Set <code>{'"permission": { "workflow": "deny" }'}</code>, or
            <code>{'"tools": { "workflow": false }'}</code>, to remove the workflow tool.
          </p>

          <h2 id="stop-and-resume">Stop, resume and restarts</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a run is one card in the Tasks pane, with a bar per stage that loads its agents when you open it. Stop on
            the card, or Stop including helpers, stops the run and its agents and keeps their work. An update, restart
            or crash never resumes a run on its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} Resume on the card, <code>vector workflow resume</code>, or the agent's workflow tool with
            <code>action: "resume"</code>, continues a run that stopped, failed or was interrupted. It reuses every
            finished agent whose stage, item, round, prompt, settings, schema and owned paths are unchanged, continues
            interrupted agents in their own sessions, reruns failed ones unless you say not to, and accepts an edited
            plan, new inputs and higher limits. Agents, spend and tokens carry over, so the ceilings hold across resumes.
            A run that is still working cannot be resumed.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} when the run ends, the conversation gets one note: the outcome, the agents, the time taken, the cost or
            "unknown", counts per stage and up to five failures, then the path of a file with the full results. A run
            you started yourself is quiet by default: its note is recorded without starting a turn, unless you ask for a
            summary.
          </p>

          <h2 id="saved-workflows">Saved workflows and trust</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a saved workflow is a JSON file in a <code>workflow/</code> or <code>workflows/</code> folder: the
            repository's <code>.vector/workflows/</code>, <code>~/.vector/workflows/</code>, or <code>workflows/</code>
            in Vector's own config folder. The file name is the workflow's name: lowercase letters, digits,
            <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>, starting with a letter or a digit, up to 64 characters. A repository's
            file wins over yours of the same name, and two names that differ only in case are both refused. An invalid
            file is listed with its problems. A stage may run another saved workflow, one level deep.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a repository's workflows load only once you trust the repository, as its agents and commands do, and a
            company lock on commands turns saved workflows off. Saved workflows are prompt files: Vector asks before an
            agent edits one, and holds one outside the repository that changed until you approve it; see
            <a href="/docs/permissions#prompt-files">Prompt files</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli">In the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} with the next CLI release, <code>vector workflow validate &lt;file&gt;</code> checks a workflow file
            without a server. Attached to one with <code>--attach</code>, <code>vector workflow saved</code> lists its
            saved workflows, and with <code>--session</code> as well, <code>list</code>, <code>show</code>,
            <code>resume</code> and <code>stop</code> manage a conversation's runs. <code>vector run --workflow &lt;name or
            file&gt;</code> runs a saved workflow or a JSON file, with <code>--input key=value</code> for its inputs: it
            prints the plan and progress to stderr and the report to stdout, records its note quietly unless you pass
            <code>--summarize</code>, and exits 1 when the run fails or is stopped. <code>vector run --workflow</code>
            and <code>vector workflow resume</code> refuse to run from an agent's shell or Vector's own terminal.
            <code>vector workflow</code> is not <code>vector memory workflow</code>, which shows Workflow insights.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector workflow validate review.json
vector run --workflow research-sweep --input topic="the HTTP client upgrade"
vector workflow show &lt;runID&gt; --attach http://localhost:4096 --session &lt;id&gt;</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>

          <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a workflow run sends nothing to Vector's service. Its agents talk to your model provider as subagents
            do, and the plan, the results and the full results file stay on your computer, in the session and in
            Vector's data folder. Guests in a shared workspace can see the runs in the sessions they can open, but never a
            run's spec, and cannot start or resume one. Runs with a workflow in them are left out of the cost
            figures behind learned defaults, because their agents may still be working when the request ends.
          </p>

          <h2 id="schema">The schema</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the workflow format is published as a JSON Schema at
            <a href="https://vectordev.ai/workflow.json">https://vectordev.ai/workflow.json</a>, so an editor can check
            a saved workflow as you write it. Vector checks every plan again itself, on every start, resume and nested
            run.
          </p>

          <h2 id="older-versions">Older engines and CLIs</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a desktop app connected to an engine without workflows, such as a Windows WSL server still on the
            engine version the desktop requires today, hides <code>/workflow</code> and the workflow controls, and the
            CLI that npm and <code>vectordev.ai/install</code> serve today has no <code>vector workflow</code> or
            <code>--workflow</code> until the next CLI release. An older desktop or TUI connected to a new engine shows
            a workflow run as a subagent named "workflow".
          </p>
</DocsPage>
